  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule. I read both back afterwards: the dashboard has all its panels, and the rule is enabled and has already run once with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view (time field `timestamp`) describes web requests. Each record has:\n- **Request:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`\n- **Outcome:** `response`, the HTTP status code, stored as a string\n- **Size:** `bytes`\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `tags`, `host`, `memory`, `phpmemory`\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-24c6ee44 Web logs overview\"** (id `ff70aa4d-8cf2-4675-8403-1456d0b77a3c`, default time range last 7 days). It has 8 panels:\n- **Headline numbers:** total requests, average bytes per request, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`) and number of 5xx server errors\n- **Requests over time:** stacked bars broken down by response code\n- **Requests by response code:** donut chart\n- **Top 10 requested URLs:** table with request count and total bytes\n- **Traffic by source country:** pie chart of `geo.src`\n\n**3. Rule: \"EFF-24c6ee44 5xx spike\"** (id `98569a93-e844-4f49-867f-366a4f56478a`). It checks `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute and fires when more than 10 requests with a 5xx response code arrive within the last 5 minutes. A request counts as 5xx when its `response.keyword` starts with \"5\".\n\nThe rule has no notification actions, so when it fires it raises an alert in Kibana but doesn't send any message. If you want emails or Slack notifications, you'll need to add a connector to the rule.",
